! ============================================================
! 05a  H2 dissociation: where the restricted picture breaks
! ============================================================
! A rigid scan of the H-H distance from 0.5 to 3.0 A.  This is
! the classic failure of restricted Hartree-Fock and it is
! worth seeing once with your own numbers.
!
! WHAT TO LOOK AT: plot the scan energies against r.
!   * near equilibrium (0.74 A) the curve is fine.
!   * at large r the RHF curve flattens out FAR TOO HIGH --
!     about 0.2 Eh above 2 x E(H atom).  RHF forces the two
!     electrons to share one spatial orbital, so at infinite
!     separation it still describes a mixture that is half
!     H(.)H(.) and half H(+)H(-).  The ionic half is what
!     costs the 0.2 Eh.
!   * the curve has the wrong SHAPE, not just the wrong
!     offset, so any property derived from it at long range --
!     a bond energy, a barrier for a dissociating bond -- is
!     wrong too.
!
! THE FIX, and the second half of the exercise: re-run with
!     %spin unrestricted
! added.  Below ~1.2 A nothing changes -- the UHF solution IS
! the RHF one there.  Beyond the Coulson-Fischer point the UHF
! solution breaks symmetry, the two electrons localize on
! opposite atoms, and the curve goes correctly to 2 x E(H).
! The price is <S^2> drifting from 0 to 1: the dissociated UHF
! state is half singlet, half triplet.
!
! There is no free lunch here.  Getting both the shape and the
! spin right needs a multireference method.
%method  HF
%basis   6-31G(d,p)
%job     scan

%gz
H1
H2 H1 0.74

%scan
R 2 from 0.50 to 3.00 step 0.10
*end
